"# Snapshot Isolation Analysis\n\n## 1. Isolation Level\n\n**Snapshot Isolation (SI)** with MVCC:\n- Each transaction reads from a consistent snapshot taken at its first read.\n- Writes are validated at commit time under **first-committer-wins**: if a concurrent transaction already committed a new version of any item in the write set, the later committer **aborts**.\n\n## 2. Assumed Interleaving\n\nThe history gives per-transaction sequences only, so I assume a start order T1, T2, T3 with overlapping execution:\n\n```\nt1:  T1.R(A)          snapshot(T1) = v0 = (A₀, B₀)\nt2:  T2.R(A)          snapshot(T2) = v0 = (A₀, B₀)\nt3:  T1.W(A)          A → A₁ (private to T1)\nt4:  T2.W(A)          A → A₂ (private to T2)\nt5:  T1.R(B)          reads B₀ (snapshot stable — repeatable)\nt6:  T2.R(B)          reads B₀\nt7:  T1.W(B)          B → B₁\nt8:  T1.COMMIT        ✓ no concurrent committed writer → A₁,B₁ become visible\nt9:  T2.W(B)          B → B₂\nt10: T2.COMMIT        ✗ ABORT — T1 concurrently committed A and B\nt11: T3.R(A)          snapshot(T3) = v1, reads A₁\nt12: T3.R(B)          reads B₁\nt13: T3.COMMIT        ✓ read-only, no validation needed\n```\n\n## 3. Serialization Graph\n\n**Committed history (T2 aborted, excluded):**\n\n```\nNodes: T1, T3\nEdges: T1 ──wr(A)──▶ T3\n       T1 ──wr(B)──▶ T3\n```\n- **Acyclic** → serializable.\n- Equivalent serial order: **T1 → T3**.\n\n**Why T2 fails — the validation edge:**\n\n```\nT1 ──ww(A)──▶ T2     (T1 committed a newer version of A in T2's write set)\nT1 ──ww(B)──▶ T2     (same for B)\n```\nFirst-committer-wins rejects T2 at t10, so these edges never enter the committed graph.\n\n**Hypothetical \"all commit\" graph (if validation were broken):**\n\n```\nT1 ──rw(A)──▶ T2     (T1 reads A₀, then T2 writes A)\nT2 ──rw(A)──▶ T1     (T2 reads A₀, then T1 writes A)   ← cycle\nT1 ⇄ T2              (same pair of edges on B)\n```\n- **Cyclic** → non-serializable. Serial equivalence would require both T1<T2 and T2<T1 — impossible.\n\n## 4. Anomalies\n\n| Anomaly | Present? | Explanation |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Lost update (ww conflict)** | ⚠️ Detected, **prevented** | T1 and T2 both read-then-write A and B from the same snapshot. First-committer-wins aborts T2. This is exactly the anomaly SI's commit validation exists to stop. |\n| Dirty read | No | MVCC hides uncommitted versions. |\n| Non-repeatable read | No | Each transaction reads one fixed snapshot. |\n| Phantom | No | Read set is a consistent snapshot. |\n| Write skew | No | T1 and T2 write the **same** items (A, B), not disjoint sets — this is a direct write-write conflict, not write skew. |\n| Read anomaly for T3 | No | T3 sees a consistent snapshot (T1's committed state). |\n\n## 5. Result\n\n- ✅ **Serializable** — but only because first-committer-wins enforcement forces **T2 to abort**. The committed history is equivalent to the serial order **T1 → T3**.\n- The history **cannot execute as written**: T2's `Commit` is a serialization failure and must retry (on retry, its new snapshot includes T1's writes, and it serializes after T1 cleanly).\n- If a system admitted all three commits (no write-write validation), the schedule would be **non-serializable**, exhibiting **lost updates** on both A and B (cycle T1 ⇄ T2 in the precedence graph) — T3 would then read a final state where one of the two transactions' updates silently vanished.\n\n**Bottom line:** under correct snapshot isolation the workload is safe but contended — with every transaction touching both A and B, only one writer commits per snapshot epoch; expect retry storms at higher concurrency. A remedy at the application level would be partitioning the write sets or moving to SELECT ... FOR UPDATE / write locks if both writers must proceed."